// A LEGAL PROGRAM THAT WAS REFUSED, AND THE DIAGNOSTIC SAID SOMETHING FALSE
// ABOUT IT. `H` implements nothing; `drop` here is an ordinary inherent method.
// borrow_check's TypeSets builder (`register_drop_symbol`) put a type into
// `ts.drop_types` on a SYMBOL SPELLING — any function whose name contains
// `__drop` — and `drop_types` is what the B87 dropck rule, the move
// classification and the loan-liveness extension all ask. So this program was
// refused with
//     binding 'g' has a `Drop` impl and borrows local 'short', ...
// a sentence that is false about a program with no `Drop` impl anywhere.
// MEASURED 2026-09-10 on `84fc082550ca57b6 43` against its one-variable
// control (`fn drop` -> `fn wipe`, every other token identical), which compiled
// rc 0. Rust: legal — `Drop` is a lang item, and dropck is about the lang item.
//
// The fix is the borrow checker's half of the same structural change that gave
// sema's `drop_fn_for` a trait package and the MLIR drop walk a trait identity:
// the LIR impl block's `identity_trait()` (always `pkg::Name`) decides, not the
// symbol's spelling.
//
// TWIN, ONE TOKEN APART: pass/bc_dropck_other_trait_named_drop_admit (the
// method moved onto a trait `Kill`).
// REFUSE TWIN: fail/bc_dropck_source_dies_first_fail — the same shape with a
// REAL `impl Drop`, which must still be refused. That pair is what separates
// "keys on the lang item" from "stopped checking".
package bc_dropck_inherent_drop_is_not_a_drop_impl_admit;
extern fn printf(fmt: *const u8, ...) -> i32;

struct H<'a> { r: &'a i64 }
impl<'a> H<'a> { fn drop(self: H<'a>) { } }

fn main() -> i32 {
    let g: H;
    { let short: i64 = 2i64; g = H { r: &short }; }
    unsafe { printf("OK\n".as_ptr()); }
    return 0i32;
}
